module gunbc.provisioning.install_target_disk

// AN UNATTENDED INSTALL ERASES WHATEVER DISK IT IS HANDED. The site PXE edge's install payload carried
// `storage: {layout: {name: direct}}`, which is the Subiquity autoinstall reference's DEFAULT behavior —
// "By default, these layouts install to the largest disk in a system"
// (https://canonical-subiquity.readthedocs-hosted.com/en/latest/reference/autoinstall-reference.html#storage)
// — so an install authorized for one machine writes whatever disk the installer happens to rank largest on
// the machine that boots: a second drive, a USB stick standing in for the target, a disk that was not the
// one anyone looked at. Operator review 2026-11-10 recorded that as a blocking safety gap: an install
// authorization must be bound to the CURRENT target-disk identity, read before actuation, and refuse if it
// no longer matches.
//
// THIS MODULE IS THAT BINDING, MODELLED — no actuation. It owns three things, and nothing else:
//   * TargetDiskReading — what a reader reported about one machine's storage, and WHEN. The reader is
//     `lsblk --output=NAME,SIZE,MODEL,SERIAL,TRAN,TYPE` (extdeps.tools.util_linux_lsblk, util-linux lsblk(8))
//     or a Redfish Storage drive (DMTF Redfish Spec DSP0266 2023.1, Services > Storage > Drives) where the
//     BMC reports them; which one is carried, because the two disagree in this fleet and a consumer must be
//     able to say so (see the rows in gunbc.fleet.machine_target_disk_observations).
//   * InstallTargetDisk — the carrier a seed is rendered from. Its only route is
//     admit_install_target_disk below, which is why the match this fleet pins is never a typed literal: a
//     caller cannot spell a serial, it can only hand over a reading and be refused or admitted.
//   * The refusal causes: no identity at all (withheld) and an identity older than the admission window
//     (refused), each carrying what it read and when, per gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT THE SEED'S DISK SELECTION — AND WHAT UNIFIES THEM. Answered here, at the change that
// introduces the second spelling, because gunbc.design_argument::single_authority makes a second place that
// independently decides one meaning a fork, and gunbc.design_argument::root_generalization makes silence the
// reject. gunbc.provisioning.ubuntu_autoinstall_seed already binds a disk by serial: TargetDiskSelector
// (ByObservedSerial | ByCatalogIdentity) matched over a probe's WHOLE population, minted only by
// select_target_disk, sealed to that route by DiskSelectionProvenance's admit_callers wall, carrying
// BoundDiskEvidence whose matched/observed counts are the proof that the rule picked exactly one disk out of
// the bay. That decision reads: WHICH DISK DOES THIS RULE PICK OUT OF THE SET THE PROBE REPORTED — zero, one
// or many, answered once from a population, with no clock in its type. The decision here is a different
// question, not a second copy of the same one: MAY THIS MACHINE'S INSTALL BE ACTUATED AT ALL, given the disk
// that reader reported and the instant the edge is about to serve it. Three consequences:
//   1. There is no population at this decision. The edge holds ONE row per machine — the disk that host's
//      reader reported — never the bay's device set. Feeding select_target_disk a one-element list would
//      mint provenance reading matched: 1 of observed: 1, asserting discriminating power over a population
//      nobody enumerated (gunbc.design_argument::epistemic_humility: assert as deduced only what is deduced).
//      It cannot be fed honestly, which is why it is not reused.
//   2. It has no time. That selection carries no read instant and no freshness bound, so the operator's
//      actual requirement — refuse when the identity is no longer current — has no arm to live in. Staleness
//      is this module's whole content, and it is absent there.
//   3. Its output is upstream's vocabulary. The PXE payload renders Subiquity's own storage.layout.match keys
//      (extdeps.os.ubuntu_autoinstall, cite-maintained); that selection produces the NoCloud seed's internal
//      binding evidence, not upstream match keys.
// THEY SHARE A ROOT — an identity-bearing disk observation admitted before an erasure — so the answer
// root_generalization asks for is the named follow-up rather than a defence: recorded as the SuspectedFork row
// `install_target_disk_binds_a_disk_beside_the_seed_selector` in gunbc.integration_concern, whose trigger is
// PXE 2c — the workflow that reads the disk identity in the same run as the boot, the first consumer to hold
// BOTH a host's enumerated disk population and a freshness-bound authorization. At that revision the question
// is decided once: either select_target_disk takes its population from admitted TargetDiskReading rows, or
// this carrier's serial becomes the evidence a DiskSelectionProvenance is minted from. Until then neither
// module may grow the other's question, and a third spelling of "the disk is the one with this serial" is a
// defect rather than a convenience.
//
// IT IS ALSO NOT THE WORKFLOW. The run that boots a client reads the disk identity in the same run as the
// boot and calls admit_install_target_disk with its own clock (that workflow is PXE 2c,
// gunbc.fleet.site_pxe_edge_converge's install route); this module is the carrier and the admission it
// consumes. And it is not a per-host thing: the readings are DATA ROWS keyed by the machine
// (gunbc.fleet.machine_target_disk_observations), never per-host declarations
// (operator ruling D2, gunbc.plans.fleet_machine_naming, gunbc#16252).

import std.types { Int, NonEmptyStr, Bool, String, EpochMs, Milliseconds }
import std.optional { Optional, Present, Absent }
import std.algebra { FreeSemigroup, non_empty_singleton, non_empty_append, non_empty_to_list, list_find_first }
import extdeps.external_authority { ExternalAuthority }
import extdeps.uri { Uri, Https }
import extdeps.storage.device_identification {
  ReportedDeviceIdentity,
  ReportedDeviceIdentityAdmitted,
  ReportedDeviceIdentityRefused,
  DeviceIdentificationRefusal,
  DeviceIdentificationToken,
  admit_reported_device_identity,
  device_identification_token_text,
  // Named, not left to the bare-reference channel: a call to an unimported name resolves by proximity and
  // detonates later at the effect join as "no established callee identity" (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode
  // bare_name_resolved_by_proximity_standing_in_for_resolution), which is why the accessors below are imported
  // even though the carrier also reads the fields directly.
  reported_device_identity_serial,
  reported_device_identity_model,
}
import extdeps.os.ubuntu_autoinstall {
  AutoinstallStorageMatchKey,
  AutoinstallDirectLayoutTarget,
  MatchedDisk,
  MatchSerial,
  MatchModel,
  autoinstall_match_key_names_one_disk,
}
import gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_identity { HostIdentity }

data gunbc_external_authority_anchor: ExternalAuthority = ExternalAuthority {
  uri: Uri {
    scheme: Https
    locator: "docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/blockdev/serial.html"
  }
}

// WHICH READER REPORTED THE DISK. Both are real tools with manuals, and which one spoke is a fact a consumer
// needs: this fleet's BMCs report an EMPTY Storage collection (artifacts/bmc/srv15-inband-recovery-2026-10-29/
// redfish_v1_Systems_system_Storage.json is `Members: []`, and srv14's first-contact capture the same), so on
// these boards the BMC does not report drives at all and the in-band lsblk reading is the only one that
// exists. Recording the source is what lets a refusal say which reader failed rather than "nothing was seen".
type DiskIdentitySource
  = LsblkBlockReport
  | RedfishStorageDrive

fn disk_identity_source_text(source: DiskIdentitySource) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match source {
    LsblkBlockReport => "lsblk"
    RedfishStorageDrive => "redfish-storage"
  }
}

// WHAT A READER SAID ABOUT ONE MACHINE'S STORAGE, AND WHEN IT SAID IT.
//
// THE READING IS TOTAL, IN THREE ANSWERS, BECAUSE EACH IS A STATE OF THE WORLD AND NOT OF THIS TYPE:
//   * DiskReported — the reader named a data-carrying disk and gave its identity. `device` is the kernel
//     device name the identity was taken from (nvme0n1 in this fleet's captures): without it the reading
//     would claim an identity without saying which disk it belongs to, and on a two-disk board that is the
//     whole question.
//   * NoDiskReported — the reader ran and named no data-carrying disk. Real here, not a placeholder: the
//     Redfish Storage collection on both of this fleet's boards answers with zero members.
//   * NotRead — nobody has read this machine's storage. This is what a machine with no row in
//     gunbc.fleet.machine_target_disk_observations reads as; it is not what srv14 is, because srv14 WAS read
//     (2026-10-16) and reported models without serials — that state is a DiskReported whose `reported_serial`
//     is Absent, and it is refused below for the reason upstream gives, not for lack of a row.
// `read_at` is on the reported answer only, because that is the only one that observed an instant; a
// fabricated timestamp on the others is exactly what gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed names as the
// witness-greening failure.
type TargetDiskReading
  = DiskReported {
      host: HostIdentity,
      device: NonEmptyStr,
      // WHAT THE READER PRINTED, which is not yet an identity. The serial and model are the strings the tool
      // emitted, because a reading is data someone recorded and an identity is something the admission below
      // decided. Had this field been the admitted token, a capture with a padded or wildcarded serial could not
      // have been written down at all — and "the tool printed something we cannot use" is a state the model has
      // to be able to name, because it is the state the operator is told about. It is also why a reading can be
      // authored outside this module while a carrier cannot: the reading is the evidence, the carrier is the
      // authorization.
      //
      // EITHER half may be Absent, because that is what the tools report: the 2026-10-16 srv14 capture ran
      // `lsblk --output=NAME,SIZE,MODEL,TRAN,TYPE` and printed a model for every disk and a serial for none.
      // Whether what was reported is ENOUGH to pin an erasure is decided by the one-disk rule below, which is
      // upstream's semantics rather than a preference — a model name is shared across every disk of that
      // model, so a model-only reading narrows a class and authorizes nothing.
      reported_serial: Optional<NonEmptyStr>,
      reported_model: Optional<NonEmptyStr>,
      source: DiskIdentitySource,
      captured_in: NonEmptyStr,
      read_at: EpochMs,
    }
  | NoDiskReported {
      host: HostIdentity,
      source: DiskIdentitySource,
      captured_in: NonEmptyStr,
      report: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | NotRead { host: HostIdentity, why: NonEmptyStr }

fn target_disk_reading_host(reading: TargetDiskReading) -> HostIdentity {
  match reading {
    DiskReported { host: host } => host
    NoDiskReported { host: host } => host
    NotRead { host: host } => host
  }
}

// THE CARRIER. Its only route is admit_install_target_disk, and it carries the reading's instant with the
// identity so that anything downstream that hands a seed a disk also sees how old the claim about it is.
// The type is a carrier in the sense gunbc.design_argument::witness_one_interface means: the seed renderer
// takes one of these and cannot take a serial, so a pinned layout is rendered FROM AN OBSERVATION or not at
// all — there is no typed-literal path to a match.
type InstallTargetDisk sole_constructor {
  host: HostIdentity
  device: NonEmptyStr
  identity: ReportedDeviceIdentity
  // THE PIN THIS CARRIER WAS ADMITTED WITH, built by admit_install_target_disk from the identity above and
  // from nothing else. It is stored rather than re-derived at render time so that the authorization and the
  // match it authorizes cannot drift apart: a carrier that got this far had a key naming ONE disk, which is
  // why the rendering path has no "what if the keys are only class-shaped" arm to get wrong.
  layout_match: FreeSemigroup<AutoinstallStorageMatchKey>
  source: DiskIdentitySource
  read_at: EpochMs
  admitted_at: EpochMs
}

fn install_target_disk_host(disk: InstallTargetDisk) -> HostIdentity {
  disk.host
}

fn install_target_disk_read_at(disk: InstallTargetDisk) -> EpochMs {
  disk.read_at
}

// WHY AN INSTALL HAS NO USABLE TARGET DISK. Every arm carries what was asked and what came back, so the
// refusal names the missing fact instead of standing in for it.
type InstallTargetDiskWithheldCause
  = TargetDiskNeverRead { host: HostIdentity, why: NonEmptyStr }
  | NoTargetDiskReported {
      host: HostIdentity,
      source: DiskIdentitySource,
      captured_in: NonEmptyStr,
      report: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | TargetDiskIdentityUnusable { host: HostIdentity, source: DiskIdentitySource, cause: DeviceIdentificationRefusal }
  // THE ONE-DISK RULE, APPLIED. The reading reported something, and every key it can be turned into names a
  // CLASS of disks rather than one disk — a model alone, or nothing at all. Upstream's own words are the
  // reason this is a refusal and not a warning: "Every supplied key must match for the disk to be a match",
  // so keys that every disk of a model satisfies leave the installer choosing, which is the state this
  // module exists to make unreachable. It carries what was reported so the operator can see the field that
  // was not enough, rather than being told the identity was bad.
  | TargetDiskMatchDoesNotBindOneDisk {
      host: HostIdentity,
      source: DiskIdentitySource,
      reported: ReportedDeviceIdentity,
    }
  | TargetDiskReadingFromTheFuture { host: HostIdentity, read_at: EpochMs, decided_at: EpochMs }
  | TargetDiskReadingStale {
      host: HostIdentity,
      read_at: EpochMs,
      decided_at: EpochMs,
      // The instant the reading stopped being current: read_at + window. EpochMs rather than Int because it
      // is an instant, and an instant reported as a bare number is a field a consumer cannot tell from a
      // duration (gunbc.design_argument::minimize_redundancy: one meaning, one name).
      deadline: EpochMs,
    }

type InstallTargetDiskAdmission
  = InstallTargetDiskAdmitted { target: InstallTargetDisk }
  | InstallTargetDiskWithheld { host: HostIdentity, cause: InstallTargetDiskWithheldCause }

// WHAT THE REPORTED IDENTITY CAN PUT IN A MATCH SPEC, IF ANYTHING. Serial first (the reference's ID_SERIAL
// key), then the model when the reading reported one (ID_MODEL) — upstream ANDs the keys it is given, so the
// model narrows what the serial already picked, and neither is a second authorization. Absent when the reading
// reported no field this spec can use: there is no key to build, and inventing one (`ssd: true`, a size) would
// be a constraint the reading never reported, which upstream would still honour — "Every supplied key must
// match for the disk to be a match" — erasing on a condition nobody observed.
//
// This is the ONE spelling of "identity in, match keys out" in the module: the admission builds the keys here,
// checks them with install_target_disk_match_binds_one_disk below, and stores the result on the carrier, so
// the rule that authorizes a disk and the keys that pin it cannot be stated twice and drift.
fn install_target_disk_identity_match_keys(
  identity: ReportedDeviceIdentity,
) -> Optional<FreeSemigroup<AutoinstallStorageMatchKey>> {
  let model_key = match reported_device_identity_model(identity: identity) {
    Absent => Absent
    Present { value: token } => Present {
      value: MatchModel { model: device_identification_token_text(token: token) },
    }
  }
  match reported_device_identity_serial(identity: identity) {
    Present { value: serial_token } => match model_key {
      Absent => Present {
        value: non_empty_singleton(
          item: MatchSerial { serial: device_identification_token_text(token: serial_token) },
        ),
      }
      Present { value: key } => Present {
        value: non_empty_append(
          left: non_empty_singleton(
            item: MatchSerial { serial: device_identification_token_text(token: serial_token) },
          ),
          right: non_empty_singleton(item: key),
        ),
      }
    }
    Absent => match model_key {
      Absent => Absent
      Present { value: key } => Present { value: non_empty_singleton(item: key) }
    }
  }
}

// THE ONE ADMISSION. `window` is the age the caller will still accept — the request's admission window, not
// a constant here, because how stale an identity may be is the actuator's policy and not this model's; the
// caller that boots the client passes the window its authorization was issued under, with its own clock
// reading as `decided_at`.
//
// THE CHECKS, IN THE ORDER THEY CAN FAIL:
//   1. Nothing was read, or nothing was reported: withheld, with the reader and its report.
//   2. The reported identity is not a usable token (extdeps.storage.device_identification): withheld. This
//      is where a serial that is really a glob — `CT*`, the reference's own example of matching a CLASS of
//      disks — is refused, so no install is authorized against a wildcard.
//   3. The keys those fields can build name no single disk: withheld (install_target_disk_match_binds_one_disk
//      below, which reads extdeps.os.ubuntu_autoinstall's upstream fact). This is where a capture that
//      reported a model and no serial is refused — the 2026-10-16 srv14 capture — instead of authorizing an
//      install against every disk of that model.
//   4. A reading from after the decision instant is not a reading of the current state: withheld. A clock
//      that runs behind its machine is a real failure mode, and a negative age must not silently count as
//      fresh.
//   5. A reading older than the window is refused: the identity is no longer the CURRENT one, which is what
//      the binding is for.
fn admit_install_target_disk(
  reading: TargetDiskReading,
  decided_at: EpochMs,
  window: Milliseconds,
) -> InstallTargetDiskAdmission {
  let host = target_disk_reading_host(reading: reading)
  match reading {
    NotRead { why: why } => InstallTargetDiskWithheld {
      host: host,
      cause: TargetDiskNeverRead { host: host, why: why },
    }
    NoDiskReported { source: source, captured_in: captured_in, report: report } => InstallTargetDiskWithheld {
      host: host,
      cause: NoTargetDiskReported {
        host: host, source: source, captured_in: captured_in, report: report,
      },
    }
    DiskReported {
      device: device,
      reported_serial: reported_serial,
      reported_model: reported_model,
      source: source,
      captured_in: _,
      read_at: read_at,
    } => match admit_reported_device_identity(
      serial: match reported_serial {
        Absent => Absent
        Present { value: text } => Present { value: text as String }
      },
      model: match reported_model {
        Absent => Absent
        Present { value: text } => Present { value: text as String }
      },
    ) {
      ReportedDeviceIdentityRefused { cause: cause } => InstallTargetDiskWithheld {
        host: host,
        cause: TargetDiskIdentityUnusable { host: host, source: source, cause: cause },
      }
      ReportedDeviceIdentityAdmitted { identity: admitted_identity } =>
        match install_target_disk_identity_match_keys(identity: admitted_identity) {
          // Nothing these fields can build, or nothing that names one disk. Both are the same failure in
          // upstream's terms: the match spec would leave the installer choosing, which is the gap this module
          // was added to close.
          Absent => InstallTargetDiskWithheld {
            host: host,
            cause: TargetDiskMatchDoesNotBindOneDisk {
              host: host, source: source, reported: admitted_identity,
            },
          }
          Present { value: keys } => if install_target_disk_match_binds_one_disk(keys: keys) {
            if decided_at < read_at {
              InstallTargetDiskWithheld {
                host: host,
                cause: TargetDiskReadingFromTheFuture { host: host, read_at: read_at, decided_at: decided_at },
              }
            } else if decided_at > read_at + window {
              InstallTargetDiskWithheld {
                host: host,
                cause: TargetDiskReadingStale {
                  host: host, read_at: read_at, decided_at: decided_at, deadline: read_at + window,
                },
              }
            } else {
              InstallTargetDiskAdmitted {
                target: InstallTargetDisk {
                  host: host,
                  device: device,
                  identity: admitted_identity,
                  layout_match: keys,
                  source: source,
                  read_at: read_at,
                  admitted_at: decided_at,
                },
              }
            }
          } else {
            InstallTargetDiskWithheld {
              host: host,
              cause: TargetDiskMatchDoesNotBindOneDisk {
                host: host, source: source, reported: admitted_identity,
              },
            }
          }
        }
    }
  }
}

// THE PIN THE CARRIER WAS ADMITTED WITH. Read off the carrier rather than rebuilt: the keys were produced by
// admit_install_target_disk from the reading's identity, and the authorization and the match it authorizes are
// therefore the same object. Nothing here can emit `ssd` or `size`, because those keys name a class of disks —
// "the largest SSD" is a selector, not an authorization — and nothing here can emit a serial the reading did
// not report. A caller that wants a pinned layout must produce an InstallTargetDisk, which has exactly one
// route.
fn install_target_disk_layout_match(disk: InstallTargetDisk) -> FreeSemigroup<AutoinstallStorageMatchKey> {
  disk.layout_match
}

// THE POLICY THIS CARRIER AUTHORIZES. The match a payload's direct layout may carry, built from the reading and
// from nothing else: this is the only function in the repository that produces a `MatchedDisk` target from a
// disk FACT, which is what makes it the authorization rather than a field someone types. An author who wants a
// pinned layout has to produce an InstallTargetDisk, and an InstallTargetDisk has exactly one route
// (admit_install_target_disk above), which is the run's decision — not a literal in a payload.
fn install_target_disk_direct_layout_target(disk: InstallTargetDisk) -> AutoinstallDirectLayoutTarget {
  MatchedDisk { keys: install_target_disk_layout_match(disk: disk) }
}

// THE DOMAIN RULE THE UPSTREAM FACT MAKES POSSIBLE, AND WHAT IT IS APPLIED TO. A match spec whose keys are all
// class-shaped — ssd, size, model, path — narrows the installer's choice without binding the install to one
// disk, so admit_install_target_disk refuses to mint a carrier from one (step 3 of its checks): this is the
// rule that stops `os_install_emit` ever rendering a `match` that leaves the installer picking the disk, which
// is the defect the operator's 2026-11-10 review named. It reads extdeps.os.ubuntu_autoinstall's
// autoinstall_match_key_names_one_disk (the upstream fact: the serial is the only key that names a device) and
// it is applied to the keys the identity can actually build — so an empty key list binds nothing, exactly as
// upstream would honour it: a match spec with no keys matches every disk in the machine.
fn install_target_disk_match_binds_one_disk(keys: FreeSemigroup<AutoinstallStorageMatchKey>) -> Bool {
  match list_find_first(xs: non_empty_to_list(keys), predicate: fn(key) {
    autoinstall_match_key_names_one_disk(key: key)
  }) {
    Present { value: _ } => true
    Absent => false
  }
}
